it was September 2006 and I found myself  sitting in a New York City Starbucks  waiting for someone I'd never met before  a couple months earlier I put my job at  the big Manhattan law firm and decided  to set out and explore new career  opportunities in doing so I jumped feet  first into the New York City tech scene  I started attending tech meetups and  going to networking events tryna just  get to know as many people in the space  that could in the process I met a  computer programmer who after hearing my  interests offered to connect me with  another person that he had recently met  it was in a similar exploratory phase of  his career so I accepted and here I was  City the Starbucks waiting for this  person Dan to arrive about 15 minutes  after I got there Dan showed up we  exchanged pleasantries we grabbed our $5  cups of coffee sat down and had what  ended up being the first of dozens of  conversations at this same Starbucks  where we swap business ideas shared  resources and generally supported one  another in our entrepreneurial efforts  my relationship with Dan over the next  seven years really influenced much of my  subsequent business success and really  solidified my belief in the power of a  strong network my name is TJ Duane I'm  an extrovert I love meeting new people  over the last 35 years  I've accumulated a lot of education and  my wife hopes that this is my last  degree but in the process I've really  come to appreciate that my education has  only done a little bit to bring me to  this point to influence my success the  people I know and those in my network  really have brought me here today it's  not what you know it's who you know it's  not always what you know it's who you  know  in fact I imagine that most of us here  in this room today are at the GSB  because we share this sentiment a lot of  us were probably motivated to come here  to broaden our professional network  however while we may recognize the value  in a strong Network sometimes we don't  always know how to do it well for  introverts networking can be a  terrifying proposition it can be  paralyzing networking situations really  can be hard to get through and they need  a lifeline to pull them along for  extroverts like me networking also has  it challenges I for one sometimes in a  little too confident in networking  situations and that is causing to step  on some some landmines along the way so  over the last 15 years I've spoken with  hundreds of students and professionals  about networking and in the process  identified a few strategies that I've  seen others use that I've tried to  employ myself some of these are like  lines that have helped me through  situations and I think would help  introverts through similar situations  others are landmines that I  unfortunately have stepped on from time  to time so I hope you'll let me share a  few of these with you today probably  those fundamental thing about a  networking interaction the first thing  that I try and do is establish a common  connection a similarity people like to  be around those who are similar to them  for introverts I usually do that sort of  for introverts the lifeline here is  identifying something early in the  conversation something you may have in  common with the other person and  establishing a connection on that  whether it's you know you've traveled to  the United Arab Emirates or you you know  you've worked in microfinance this  Harshman and and Rob have or whatever it  may be find that connection early  establish that similarity and it'll make  you memorable for that person it's  something that I value the landmine here  for extroverts is being a bit too  confident or too comfortable or making  assumptions too early I used to have a  colleague who was recently divorced and  would start out engagements or  interactions by mentioning that fact  thinking it would like engender sympathy  let's just say it was a bit awkward next  be centered you know be focused be in  the moment  all too often we're in networking  situations and there's a lot a lot else  going on in fact you know just last  evening I was having coffee with Camilla  and we were in town square and there  were so many people walking around that  I would find myself sort of looking over  her shoulder glancing around the  landmine that's a real landmine you know  don't be  don't take your attention from the  person that you're engaging with don't  wave and nod at someone walking by it  shows that you're not really in the  moment like one here or introverts could  be you know taking the person's name  remembering their name appreciating who  you're talking to and using their name  in the conversation it will show that  you're you care and that you're you're  in the moment and you're focused in  addition you know listening to their  story why are we in these networking  situations we're in these networking  situations because we want to broaden  you know the people that we know and  Huson add value to our careers and our  lives so you want to know how this  person's gonna be able to add value to  you you want to listen to their story  and sometimes it's hard for people to  tell their story  so the lifeline here is you know ask  questions sincere questions draw out  their story their expertise identify  ways that they'll be able to add value  for you the landmine here as I've been  known to do in the past is asking too  many questions this is a conversation  it's a networking interaction it's not  an interview and on occasion I may have  veered into the realm of interrogation  definitely not an interrogation I've  been talking people who've hit me with  like gunfire questions one after another  it's a little bit of a turn-off and I  know I've done it myself so that's it's  something totally in addition to  listening to their story and seeing how  they can add value to your network you  want to make it clear how you're gonna  add value to their network why do people  network again it's to see how they can  broaden their influence and  and the value that people can add so in  speaking with someone a lifeline here is  you know telling your story getting it  into the conversation you know talking  about your experience your expertise  your education the different things that  you know that may be valuable to them  the landmine of course is telling your  story too enthusiastically sometimes  sometimes talking about your expertise  and your experience can come across as  bragging we've all talked to people who  early on in the interaction with them  they talked about all the great places  they visited and the houses they owned  and the cars they owned and all this  expertise they have and it can be a bit  of a turn-off so be mindful that fifth  just as important is starting out the  conversation by established by  establishing similarity is booked ending  the conversation  with a follow-up lifeline and following  up and following through a lifeline here  clearances try to identify something in  the conversation where you can add  immediate value you know think of when  personally when you're talk with the  person think of a way that you me may be  able to make a connection for them  straight away much the same way that  computer program I did with me and Dan  seven years ago if you can add value  immediately do it if not you can add  value  soon after do that as well one thing I  like to do is if I'm reading a newspaper  article or seeing a you know I see a  product that I know someone in my  network may be interested in I'll go  ahead and send it to them in fact you  know just yesterday Sabrina and I met  with the CLO over Evernote and I was  great great meeting was for a class  project and this morning in the Wall  Street Journal there was a quote from  the the co-founder the chips for the  co-founder of the charter schools hip  charter schools saying how he loved  Evernote it was his favorite app I was  the one to use all the time so what I do  this morning I shot an email off to the  CLO saying hey look you got some you got  a nice shout out in The Wall Street  Journal this morning it shows people  that you were in the moment in that  networking engagement you're thinking  about them and you're you know you're  being mindful about what they may need  and you're showing how again you can add  value following through is super  important we've all been in situations  where someone says they're gonna do  something and they don't someone says  they're gonna call and they don't that  doesn't work in the dating world doesn't  work in the networking world don't over  promise and under deliver follow up  follow through so these are just a few  strategies to help make networking  situations one-on-one networking  situations a little bit easier a  big-picture networking isn't  transactional it's not about numbers  it's not about how many LinkedIn  connections you have or how many  Facebook friends you have networking is  about building real relationships  relationships where you add value and  the other person adds value for you I  didn't know seven years ago when I sat  down with Dan that first time that the  conversations we were gonna have and the  ventures we were gonna work on together  would one day lead me to stand here  today but they did and the relationship  with Dan and dozens others over the  years have put me in a position to be  able to speak with you this  for some of you in the room maybe a  little shocked more shy I hope some of  these landmines that I discussed will  help make the next networking  interaction for you a bit more  comfortable for some of you who are  extrovert seen maybe a bit overzealous  like myself I help talking about some of  the landmines I've stepped on will prove  cautionary in the end when we're  finished with the GSB we'll all have  accumulated a lot of Education long know  a lot and although that education will  take us a certain distance the people we  know those in our network the  relationships we've built will determine  our ultimate success thank you
 

